# Rank 1881 / CVE-2021-27292: ua-parser-js's Barnes & Noble tablet regex
# let a greedy dot-star and the whitespace run after it share every space, so
# a user agent such as "android; BNVT " followed by tens of thousands of
# spaces and no "build" costs quadratic backtracking inside the device mapper
# (CWE-1333 inefficient regular expression complexity, CWE-400).
#
# The vulnerable literal at src/ua-parser.js:623 (fix parent) is
# /android.+[;\/]\s+(Barnes[&\s]+Noble\s+|BN[RT])(V?.*)\s+build/i. The third
# group's .* admits every whitespace character and the \s+ that follows the
# group matches whitespace too, so on a failing subject the engine can hand
# each space to either quantifier and walks the O(n^2) set of splits. The fixed revision
# 809439e2 rewrites the group to (\S(?:.*\S)?) -- the group now starts and
# ends on a non-space, no split of the space run is ambiguous -- and changes
# nothing else (the second hunk drops a trailing comma after /(phone)/i, a
# syntax cleanup). The whole discriminator is the quantifier structure of the
# pattern text. The pool row reads correctly: the change is targeted, the
# vulnerable literal is at the parent, the remediation is at the commit. No

#
# The regexes are applied by mapper.rgx (src/ua-parser.js:98):
# `matches = regex[j++].exec(ua);` -- each literal sits in a module-scope
# array of [regexes, props] pairs and is applied receiver-side through an
# element subscript, with ua the user-agent string UAParser is constructed
# with. No argument-position application of these literals exists anywhere in
# the module.
#
# ATTENTION, engine gap, and the whole path is blocked by the family rank
# 1864 recorded in ENGINE-GAPS (the JavaScript instance of the 1343
# polynomial cost-model boundary), restated for this shape:
#
# First, regexambig.Ambiguous answers false -- correctly for what it models:
# the pattern has no nested overlapping quantified branch, and the ambiguity
# here is adjacent (a quantifier pair whose classes intersect), the class the
# 1343 entry records as needing a polynomial cost model plus an
# operand-boundedness notion before any of its candidate shapes is
# reportable.
#
# Second, the frontend's own adjacent-quantifier analysis never sees the
# pair. jsRegexAtoms (internal/extract/frontend/treesitter/javascript.go:1165)
# lowers (V?.*) to a single opaque "group" atom with no quantifier, so the
# \s+ after the closing paren has nothing to overlap; and its default case
# (javascript.go:1223) skips . and * outright without emitting an atom, so
# regexAtomKey's "." -> "any" mapping and regexAtomsOverlap's a == "any" ||
# b == "any" disjunct are dead code -- no atom ever carries key "any". A
# probe confirms the consequence beyond the group case: even the ungrouped
# /^.*\s+(\S+\s+\S+$)/, the plainest adjacent-overlap spelling with no group
# boundary to hide behind, synthesises no __regex.match node. vyql graph on
# the real shape reports the literal as a plain code.Const.
#
# Third, even where the analysis does fire, ua-parser's application route
# reports nothing. The nested-ambiguity control below ((\d+)*m substituted
# into the same group) does synthesise a __regex.match node at module scope
# -- the synthesis machinery works for the nested class -- but no JavaScript
# binding consumes __regex.match (Ruby's regexMatch205 has no JS twin), and
# the receiver-applied .exec route carries no RegexMatchSink in any case:
# vyql match on the control reports only the known code.CommandExecution
# mislabel on the .exec argument. The same measurement at rank 1864: the
# identical nested literal applied through .exec scans to zero while the
# same literal as a .replace argument reports.
#
# A binding cannot bridge it instead. The only discriminating datum is the
# pattern text, and the class shape -- a dot quantifier immediately followed
# by a whitespace quantifier, \.[*+] then \s[*+] modulo a group close --
# enumerates to 5 occurrences in 5 files across the 1631 sibling JavaScript
# files of the 75 concurrent checkouts (OpenLayers' typeStr
# /^\s*(\w+)\s*\(\s*(.*)\s*\)\s*$/, traefik's bundled /\(\s*(.*)\s*\)/g,
# typebot's /^(.*?)\s*\(\d+\)$/, easyWebSocket's /^.*\s+(\S+\s+\S+$)/), none
# of them a known weakness -- 1 real in 6, on top of the 1343 census's 1
# real in 7-11 for exactly this route and 1864's ten-files-for-one census of
# the sibling shape. The taint-gated rule cannot rescue it either: VYQL-DOS-002
# needs the sink where the operand is applied, the literal is applied through
# regex[j++] and no route links a module-scope array element to the receiver
# of a distant .exec, so no source-to-sink path exists -- vyql trace from
# code.ExternalEntryInput to code.RegexMatchSink over the real shape reports
# 0 sources, 0 dead-ends. Concepts considered and unreachable or unshippable:
# code.CatastrophicRegex (the ungated route the census kills),
# code.RegexMatchSink and code.RegexMatch (need a call-site label this
# application never produces). Nothing minted.
#
# Measured on the real checkout, both revisions, timeout 180 --max-ram 6GB
# --cache off: 1 finding at the vulnerable revision and 1 at the fixed revision, three runs each
# at the parent, identical fingerprint (VYQL-INJ-013 ProtoPollute on
# this[q] = match in mapper.rgx, src/ua-parser.js:932, fp c65dc029b8c09077),
# zero discrimination in both directions and nothing DOS-related at either
# revision.
#
# Not mechanical for the reasons the 1864 entry records: a class-intersection
# overlap test, a live dot atom, or a polynomial cost case are each a new
# classification with scan-wide effect on what gets labelled, and the 1343
# measurement says the obvious forms are not reportable without the
# operand-boundedness notion. The pair below pins the boundary with no
# ua-parser API: the same exported constructor applying its regex array
# reports nothing whether the third group carries this CVE's adjacent
# overlap or a nested ambiguity that the cost model does classify, while the
# same module's argument-position replace idiom with a classified pattern
# reports VYQL-DOS-002 -- the two legs differ only in the quantifier
# structure of the pattern and in whether the literal reaches its
# application as a call argument.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-engine-gap, both revisions rejected.
# When a polynomial backtracking classification lands together with a
# JavaScript __regex.match consumer (or a receiver-route label), the first
# test flips to expect VYQL-DOS-002 and the second stays reject.

test "rank1881: the Barnes & Noble device regex splits a space run between a greedy dot-star and the following space quantifier"
  lang javascript
  file src/ua-parser.js
  profile library
  reject VYQL-DOS-002
  reject VYQL-DOS-003
  code
  ```
  var regexes = [
      [
      /android.+[;\/]\s*(Q[T|M][\d\w]+)\s+build/i                         // Verizon Tablet
      ], ['model', ['vendor', 'Verizon'], ['type', 'tablet']], [

      /android.+[;\/]\s+(Barnes[&\s]+Noble\s+|BN[RT])(V?.*)\s+build/i     // Barnes & Noble Tablet
      ], [['vendor', 'Barnes & Noble'], 'model', ['type', 'tablet']], [

      /(phone)/i
      ], [['type', 'mobile']]
  ];

  var mapper = {

      rgx : function (ua, arrays) {

          var i = 0, j, matches;

          while (i < arrays.length && !matches) {

              var regex = arrays[i];
              j = 0;

              while (j < regex.length && !matches) {

                  matches = regex[j++].exec(ua);

                  if (!!matches) {
                      this['model'] = matches[1];
                  }
              }
              i += 2;
          }
      }
  };

  function UAParser(uastring) {

      var ua = uastring || '';

      mapper.rgx.call(this, ua, regexes);

      return this;
  }

  module.exports = UAParser;
  ```

