# The isolating pair. Same exported constructor, same module-scope regex
# array, same receiver-applied exec: only the quantifier structure of the
# third group differs. The nested-overlap class is classified by the shared
# cost model and synthesises a node; the adjacent-overlap class that is this
# CVE does not -- and through this application route neither reports,
# because no JavaScript binding consumes __regex.match and the .exec
# receiver carries no sink. The fourth test applies a classified pattern as
# a call argument in the same module shape (util.trim's argument-position
# replace idiom) and is reported, isolating that the miss is the quantifier
# class and the application route, not the source gate.

test "rank1881 pair: the nested-overlap quantifier class through the same array-applied exec is not reported"
  lang javascript
  file src/ua-parser.js
  profile library
  reject VYQL-DOS-002
  reject VYQL-DOS-003
  code
  ```
  var regexes = [
      [
      /android.+[;\/]\s+(Barnes[&\s]+Noble\s+|BN[RT])((\d+)*m)\s+build/i     // nested-ambiguity control
      ], [['vendor', 'Barnes & Noble'], 'model', ['type', 'tablet']]
  ];

  var mapper = {

      rgx : function (ua, arrays) {

          var i = 0, j, matches;

          while (i < arrays.length && !matches) {

              var regex = arrays[i];
              j = 0;

              while (j < regex.length && !matches) {

                  matches = regex[j++].exec(ua);

                  if (!!matches) {
                      this['model'] = matches[1];
                  }
              }
              i += 2;
          }
      }
  };

  function UAParser(uastring) {

      var ua = uastring || '';

      mapper.rgx.call(this, ua, regexes);

      return this;
  }

  module.exports = UAParser;
  ```

